From 662ed4ba382612ec8aa17c6bf1733aaba72aeabf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shapey Review <review@shapey.local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 08:20:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] test(audience): point the audience frontend at a local
 Worker for testing

The audience page hard-coded MULTIPLAYER_API to the deployed staging
Worker, so every audience check necessarily exercised the deployed
service instead of the refactored local Worker.

- staging/audience/state.js gains the same test-only override the game
  client already has (src/config.js): a page served from localhost or
  127.0.0.1 may pass ?api=http://127.0.0.1:8787 to talk to a local
  Worker. The parameter is ignored on every other origin, so the
  deployed audience configuration can never be redirected; the URL
  stays hard-coded as the default and the staging-preview workflow's
  grep of state.js still finds it.
- The embedded game frame, the copied watch link and the leave-room
  navigation carry the override while local testing is active, so a
  reload (or a shared link) stays pointed at the local Worker.
- worker/audience-smoke.mjs runs the audience frontend end to end
  against the local refactored Worker: room discovery in the live-games
  directory, joining and spectating (embedded game frame + spectator
  counts), chat delivery between two audience devices, the four-player
  two-team Tag roster and turn rotation, poll voting from the board
  tiles with server-side expiry and result reveal, and audience plus
  player reconnect - with one audience device's clock skewed 40s to
  prove the audience-side countdowns stay correct. Idle player pages
  park their render loops (game.loop.sleep) so the six concurrent
  Phaser instances cannot starve the embedded game on a CI runner.
- Both new browser smokes (clock-skew + audience) are wired into the
  worker-tests workflow in their own CI job: the Worker rate-limits
  room creation (5 rooms per 10 minutes per client - one shared bucket
  for a local wrangler dev), and the integration/browser/tag smokes
  already create exactly five rooms between them, so the new smokes get
  their own ephemeral Worker state and bucket. worker/README.md
  documents all four browser smokes.

- Both smokes anchor the poll phase on a real match shape (a Cancel
  pick clears the selection 350ms later, which would make a poll
  impossible) and retry the polls-button press until the poll actually
  opens - a single press can be swallowed by the pick flip animation
  or a momentarily reconnecting socket, and a real player simply
  presses again. waitForScene's timeout dump now also reports the
  socket state, the selected-tile count and the polls used, so any
  future flake is diagnosable from the log alone. The audience smoke's
  Tag handover likewise accepts a turn the server already rotated at
  its authoritative deadline: the audience-side rotation view is the
  subject under test, and the audience phases can consume much of the
  30-second Tag turn on a loaded runner; a broken submit button is
  still caught on any fresh turn, and tag-browser-smoke covers the
  button's causal effect independently.
